# The Hermes checkout is 6.4 GB. Almost none of it belongs in an image.
# Measured 2026-08-18: 3.5 GB in hermes-agent (mostly its venv and node_modules),
# 1.1 GB in .git. Without this file the build context alone would take minutes to
# send to the daemon and the image would be unshippable.

.git
.git/**
**/.git

# Rebuilt inside the image against Linux wheels. A macOS venv is not portable and
# copying one in produces an interpreter that cannot import anything.
**/venv
**/.venv
**/node_modules
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc

# Runtime state. It lives on the Fly volume at /data, never in the image.
logs/
state/
runs/
receipts/
*.log
*.db
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
# The sidecars too. `*.db` does not match `state.db-wal`, so the 2026-08-18 image
# shipped a 1.9 MB write-ahead log with no database behind it, and Hermes ran for
# 72 minutes on it. Ship neither, or ship both; never one.
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
*.sqlite-wal
*.sqlite-shm

# Secrets never enter an image layer. They arrive as `fly secrets`, which are
# environment variables at runtime and are not part of any layer a registry holds.
.env
.env.*
secrets.age
.lux/keys/

# Local-only
.DS_Store
deploy/**/data

# Cron job OUTPUT, not the job definitions. 185MB of logs the image has no use for.
cron/output/

# Local working directories that are not part of the running agent. Measured
# 2026-08-19 during a release: the build context reached 900 MB and was still
# climbing after three and a half minutes. backups/ (278 MB), .worktrees/
# (177 MB) and repomix-output.xml (72 MB) are the bulk of it, and nothing in
# deploy/hermes/ references any of them. tests/test_docker_context_is_bounded.py
# is what fails the next time a big directory appears here unlisted.
backups/
.worktrees/
cache/
repomix-output.xml

# macOS binaries. `bin/uv` is a Mach-O x86_64 executable, so it cannot run in the
# Linux image at all — 71 MB of context that does nothing, and a copy of uv the
# container might mistake for an installed one. hermes_cli/config.py:2535 says
# these are fetched into ~/.hermes/bin on first use anyway.
bin/
